y building out technological  solutions to support open practice  and the next is this idea of public  public science which is about the  accessibility of knowledge creation  particularly for publicly funded  research and making that research  accessible to the public that funded it  as well as bringing the public in  through citizen science efforts which  means normal everyday people can  actually contribute to scientific  practice to research as it's ongoing  then there's the measurement school  which is about alternative impacts and  essentially this takes into account the  fact that we did the rise of digital  technologies the way that you measure  what your research has impacted what it  is done has shifted social media metrics  are an example of alternative in fact  measurement there's a democratic school  which is related to the public but it's  more global is this idea that access to  scientific information is a human right  it's something that's cross national  international it's across countries it's  across communities within a country  across socio-economic barriers and it's  fundamentally about the access to  knowledge which means not only making it  available but making it understandable  by all of these different groups so  enabling that translation and finally  there's a pragmatic school which  basically just takes into account the  fact that a lot of scientific problems  require a lot of brainpower more than  you can house under one roof in one lab  it's too expensive too maybe the skill  sets aren't all in one place at the same  time so the pragmatic school is about  taking into account these new challenges  from the difficulty of conducting some  kinds of research and just bringing  people together to make it happen and  again this has been really big in the  news lately a useful way to consent  contextualize all these broad ideas is  through newspaper articles and this is  going back to cancer reproducibility  study it was really big news in the 