w do we draw people  together from our own  organization in this case the ncsu  libraries to help contribute learning  materials to this project there are a  lot of approaches that when one could  take to sort of support this sort of  learning environment you could create  custom operating system images which end  up being pretty large may be hard to  distribute you could lean on custom  distributions like anaconda which is  marvelous of course or you could use the  really rich interactive environments  like Jupiter which we saw a lot of great  success with I think yesterday and today  the approach we elected to take was to  take a look at some virtualization tools  designed for developers and provisioning  tools designed for server management and  configuration so we used vagrant and  ansible together to help us create  lightweight localized repeatable  reproducible computing environments that  were easier for us to maintain because  the outputs of them rather than being a  large disk image were just text files  that we could put into our enterprise  github system and edit and share very  easily it's a quick show of hands how  many people have used vagrant or heard  of it how many people have used ansible  or something similar chef puppet yes  great so quick introduction for those  that aren't familiar vagrant is really  at its simplest a tool for helping to  coordinate virtual images often through  VirtualBox their ad copy says you can  create and configure lightweight  reproducible and portable development  environments and it's extremely easy to  use there's a binary installer that you  can put on your machine once you've  installed vagrants it will also install  VirtualBox you type vagrant in it and it  creates an initialization file that's  just simply a text file written in Ruby  yeah that's right in Ruby and then you  run a single command vagrant up it reads  that file and your environment is  scaffold assuming that you've plugged it  in with a provisioner a